Insert Curved and Straight Segments
You can insert a Track, a TwoLaneTrack, a Conveyor, a FootPath, or a Pipe as a sequence of curved and
straight segments into the Frame that contains your simulation model.
Proceed as follows:
• Zoom the Frame to a size that it can accommodate the object you want to insert.
• Click the length-oriented object in the Toolbox, and drag the mouse to the Frame window and click the
point where the object is to start. This opens the dialog Edit Parameters of Curve.
To draw a straight segment, click a second time at the position at which you want the end of the
segment to be. To draw a curved segment, hold down Ctrl, drag the mouse down to draw the curve
downward, and click the left mouse button once to set the curve.
Insert Curved and Straight Segments
Plant Simulation Help 10-933
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To draw the next straight segment of the line, release Ctrl, drag the mouse to the end point of the line
and click the left mouse button once.
To draw a continuous sequence of straight and curved segments, for example for continuous conveyors,
click an existing curved or straight end segment to start a new segment. Plant Simulation then uses the
end coordinate of the first sequence as the start coordinate of the second sequence and automatically
connects the two. You can prevent this by holding down Alt.
Click the right mouse button once or click Finish 
  to exit Insert mode and to insert the curve
you drew. This also closes the dialog Edit Parameters of Curve.
Press Esc or click Abort 
  to terminate curve mode without inserting the curve.
Click Delete Last Point 
  to delete the most recently inserted anchor point.
Insert Curved and Straight Segments
10-934 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

When the grid is active 
 , Plant Simulation places the points that make up the line onto grid points,
provided this is possible. For this, fixed values take precedence. When you type in a radius of 2.5 meters,
for example, the end point of the arc will not be placed onto a grid point. This results in a non-tangential
transition between the end of the arc and the end of the next straight segment, which will be placed
onto a grid point again.
When you insert a straight segment after a curved segment, Plant Simulation always enforces a
tangential angle of 0°.
To insert the length-oriented curved object with fixed values, type them into the text boxes in the dialog
and select the check box fixed of the respective item to tell Plant Simulation that you want to use these
fixed values.
◦ For a line segment, you can type in the Line Length in world coordinates and the Tangential Angle
as compared to the previous segment.
Insert Curved and Straight Segments
Plant Simulation Help 10-935
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

For both segment types you can type in an Anchor Point Height. It determines the height, i.e., the
distance from the floor, to the next anchor point. It is, for example, useful when you model a ramp for
material handling equipment.
◦ For a curved segment, you can type in the Arc Length, its Radius, and its Curve Angle in degrees as
compared to the previous segment.
The Curve Angle is the angle that is formed by the radius between the start point A and the end
point B of the arc segment. A positive number designates a clockwise angle. A negative number a
counterclockwise angle.
Insert Curved and Straight Segments
10-936 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
When you define a curve angle of 360°, Plant Simulation automatically connects the end point the
curve with its starting point.
◦ For a following Straight Segment you can type in the Tangential Angle and the Line Length.
The tangential angle designates the change in the tangential direction. Type in a positive number
for a clockwise angle. Type in a negative number for a counterclockwise angle. Normally the
tangential angle is relative to the previous direction of the curved segment. For the first segment the
tangential angle is the angle to the positive x direction.
When you insert a straight segment after a curved segment, Plant Simulation always enforces a
tangential angle of 0 degrees.
Insert Curved and Straight Segments
Plant Simulation Help 10-937
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Plant Simulation creates the start tangential angle as follows:
After clicking an existing object with the mouse, Plant Simulation applies the angle from the previous
object as start tangential angle. This also applies to icons.
When setting a straight segment by a mouse click on an existing object, Plant Simulation uses,
depending on the mouse position, either the angle of the object or +-90 degrees to this angle as the
start tangential angle.
When setting a curved segment without clicking an existing object, Plant Simulation proceeds like
this: When you type in an angle for straight segments, it uses this angle as the start tangential
angle. When you hold down Ctrl, Plant Simulation uses, depending on the mouse position, 0, +-90 or
180 degrees as the start tangential angle.
When you insert a length-oriented curved object, Plant Simulation shows the extrusion path of this object.
The path of the polycurve initially just consists of the starting point:
• Either the insertion point, i.e., the position on the grid on which you click or
• The end point of the picked object, which is its exit projected onto the XY layer.
• Plant Simulation shows a movable line, which connects a manipulator at the starting point with another
manipulator attached to the cursor.
• You insert straight path segments by clicking the left mouse button. You insert curved path segments by
holding down Shift and by clicking the left mouse button.
• You terminate curve mode by clicking the right mouse button or by pressing Esc.
• Plant Simulation will not insert a length-oriented object, if the curved object does not consist of at least
two segments/points after you finished or terminated inserting the curved object.
Compare Add Animations and Interactions to the Simulation Objects
Compare Edit a Curved Path with the Mouse
Back to Work with Length-oriented Objects
Insert Curved and Straight Segments
10-938 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Auto-connect Length-oriented Objects
When you define a length-oriented object and click another object with the mouse to set that object as
the successor of the length-oriented object, Plant Simulation attempts to automatically bridge the gap
from the current end of the length-oriented object to the successor with additional straight and/or curved
segments.
How Plant Simulation Bridges a Gap
To bridge the gap, Plant Simulation tries to create additional line and/or curve segments so that the
resulting length-oriented object connects its predecessor object and its successor object tangentially.
The predecessor and the successor can be a length-oriented object, a point-oriented object, or no object at
all. The direction to be continued tangentially is determined by the last/first segment of a length-oriented
predecessor/successor, the X-Axis of a point-oriented successor, or the X-Axis of the scene.
Plant Simulation automatically corrects any existing difference in height in the last segment of the
constructed length-oriented object.
Plant Simulation can only close a gap under the following conditions:
• The Curve Angle in the Line/Arc Parameters dialog is fixed to 90 degrees and the Radius is fixed as
well.
• The objects, which you want to connect, are aligned in parallel or perpendicular to each other.
• The gap can be closed with maximally two right-angled curve segments.
• The objects, which you want to connect, are not rotated around any other axis than the z-axis.
Auto-connect Length-oriented Objects
Plant Simulation Help 10-939
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Meeting the above conditions, Plant Simulation can bridge a gap in the following cases. The objects do not
necessarily have to be aligned to the grid:
• The objects, which you want to connect, are aligned to each other and point in the same direction.
• The objects, which you want to connect, are positioned parallel to each other and point in the same
direction. Both the tangential and vertical distance of the connection points is at least the double radius
of the Line/Arc Parameters.
• The objects, which you want to connect, are positioned parallel to each other but point in the opposite
direction. The vertical distance of the connection points is at least the double radius of the Line/Arc
Parameters.
• The objects, which you want to connect, are placed with a right angle to each other. Both the tangential
and vertical distance of the connection points is at least the radius of the Line/Arc Parameters.
Auto-connect Length-oriented Objects
10-940 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

If Plant Simulation cannot close the gap under these conditions, it suggests a straight line pointing directly
to the connection point of the successor object, provided you did not hold the Ctrl key down.
You can then decide what you want to do:
• Connect the length-oriented object with segments along the suggested polyline by clicking the
successor object.
• Move the cursor away from the successor object and manually define separate tangentially prolongating
segments, compare Insert Curved and Straight Segments.
How to Bridge and Close the Gap
If Plant Simulation can bridge and close the gap with the picked successor object, then it shows the
computed polyline as a preview as soon as you drag the cursor over the successor object.
Click the successor, to close the gap and establish the connections.
Auto-connect Length-oriented Objects
Plant Simulation Help 10-941
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Back to Work with Length-oriented Objects
Draw Straight and Curved Segments with a 90° Angle
You can draw a length-oriented curved object with a straight segment,and a 90° curve followed by another
straight or curved segment.
Proceed as follows:
• Click the icon 
  if the Conveyor in the Toolbox.
• Drag the mouse to the Frame window to the point where you would like to start the straight segment of
the line.
• Click the left mouse button once to set the starting point of the line.
Draw Straight and Curved Segments with a 90° Angle
10-942 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Plant Simulation opens the dialog Edit Parameters of Curve. Here, we defined default settings for
inserting a curve with a 90° angle, i.e., a fixed tangential angle of 0°, a fixed curve angle of 90° and a
fixed radius of 2 meters.
You can either apply these values or you can type in a different radius to make the curve wider. To do so,
drag the mouse to the dialog and type another value into the text box.
Note
Plant Simulation saves the last set of settings in the dialog, so that you can re-use them the next time
you insert a curved object.
To draw the curve, drag the mouse back to the Frame window, hold down Ctrl, drag the mouse down to
draw the curve downward, and click the left mouse button once to set the curve.
To draw a curve pointing to the left: Drag the mouse back to the Frame window and hold down Ctrl.
Make sure that Fixed for the Curve angle of the arc segments is activated and type in 90. Then drag the
mouse down to draw the curve downward, and click the left mouse button once to set the curve.
Draw Straight and Curved Segments with a 90° Angle
Plant Simulation Help 10-943
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To draw the next straight segment of the line, release Ctrl, drag the mouse to the end point of the line
and click the left mouse button once.
• Click the right mouse button once to exit Insert mode. This also closes the dialog Edit Parameters of
Curve.
Draw Straight and Curved Segments with a 90° Angle
10-944 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

When the grid is active 
 , Plant Simulation places the points that make up the line onto grid points,
provided this is possible. For this fixed vales take precedence. When you type in a radius of 2.5 meters,
for example, the end point of the arc will not be placed onto a grid point. This results in a non-tangential
transition between the end of the arc and the end of the next straight segment, which will be placed onto
a grid point again.
To get a tangential transition, you can:
• Deactivate Snap to Grid by holding down Alt, when you click the left mouse button to set the end point
of the second straight segment.
• Type in a Tangential angle of 0 degrees and set it as fixed for this straight segment. Then deactivate
this setting for the following straight segments.
Draw Straight and Curved Segments with a 90° Angle
Plant Simulation Help 10-945
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To draw a curved segment without using fixed values, you have to click the left mouse button three times.
To activate curve mode, hold down Ctrl, when you click the mouse button the first time!
• The first mouse click sets the starting point.
• The second mouse click sets the radius.
• The third mouse click sets the arc length or the curve angle respectively.
Back to Work with Length-oriented Objects
Draw Straight and Curved Segments without Fixed Values
You can draw a straight and a curved segment of a length-oriented curved object without using fixed
values.
Proceed as follows:
• To set the starting point of the segment, click the left mouse button once. You will notice that Plant
Simulation attaches the icon of the Connector to the front end of the cursor 
  in the Frame window.
The text boxes now show the actual values of the line segment.
• Drag the mouse in a direction of your choice and as far as you want the segment to be long. Click the
left mouse button again to set the first anchor point. Now, Plant Simulation draws the segment with the
settings you selected.
Draw Straight and Curved Segments without Fixed Values
10-946 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• Continue this until the object has the length and the shape you want.
• To set the end point of the segment, and to exit Insert mode, click the right mouse button.
To draw a curved segment of a length-oriented curved object without using fixed values:
• To set the starting point of the curved segment, hold down Ctrl and click the left mouse button once.
You will notice that the text boxes now show the actual values of the curve segment.
• To set the Tangential Angle, drag the mouse in a direction of your choice.
• To set the Radius of the curved segment continue dragging the mouse until the curve has the radius
you want it to be and click the left mouse button.
• To set the Arc Length, continue dragging the mouse until the curve is as long as you want it to be and
click the left mouse button.
• To set the end point of the segment, click the right mouse button.
• To delete the last anchor point from the curve, drag the mouse pointer from the Frame window to the
dialog Edit Parameters of Curve and click Delete Last Point.
Naturally you can combine both techniques to insert curved and straight segments in any sequence you
like.
Back to Work with Length-oriented Objects
Change the Shape of a Segment
You can modify the shape of a segment in a number of ways.
Proceed as follows:
• You can change the shape of a straight segment by clicking Show Manipulators and by then dragging
the manipulators.
• To delete an anchor point from the curve, right-click it and select Delete Anchor Point on the context
menu.
• To extend an existing curved segment of the length-oriented object without adding a new anchor point,
click the drag point with the left mouse button and drag the mouse to the new position.
Change the Shape of a Segment
Plant Simulation Help 10-947
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To append anchor points to the end of the curve, right-click anywhere on the curve and select
Segments > Append [segment] on the context menu. Add an additional anchor point with the dialog
Edit Parameters of Curve.
• To edit existing segments and to insert new segments, select Segments > Edit [segment]. In the
Segments Table you can also insert a segment at the beginning of the object which is not possible
manually with the mouse.
In the example below we insert a curved segment at the beginning of the Pipe so that it seamlessly
attaches to the FluidSource.
◦ Click the Pipe and press F7.
◦ This opens the Segments table. Click in row 2 and select Insert Row. This inserts an empty row above
row 2.
We typed in these values:
◦ The result looks like this. We then have to adjust the position of the Pipe.
Change the Shape of a Segment
10-948 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• To change the direction of a curved object, for example from right to left, edit the Curve angle in the
Segments table. Our Conveyor1 evades an obstacle to the right.
The Curve angles in the Segments table look like this.
Our Conveyor2 is to evade the obstacle to the left. To accomplish this, we changed the Curve angles in
the Segments table as shown below.
Change the Shape of a Segment
Plant Simulation Help 10-949
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

This then looks like this:
• To replace the right angle of a curved object with a curve, we are going to edit the Segments table. We
originally inserted this Footpath with a right angle. We now want the Worker to walk along a curve.
We press F7 to open the Segments table of the Footpath. It looks like this:
Change the Shape of a Segment
10-950 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To see how we have to change the Segments table of our Footpath, we insert a second Footpath with a
curve instead of the right angle above our Footpath. Footpath1 and its Segments table look like this:
Then left-click the Footpath and press F7. Right-click in the last row of the Segments table and select
Insert Row to insert an empty row before it. Type in the values that our curved Footpath1 has. Click
Apply. Our Footpath now has a curve instead of a right-angle.
You can then delete Footpath1 which just provided the template for our curve.
• To change the direction of motion of a curved object, i.e., to make the last point of the object the first
point, right-click the object and select Reverse [segment]. This is especially handy if you want to mirror
the layout of a production line by copying it and then reversing the direction of motion of the mirrored
line to ensure a circular flow of materials.
